XR500v dual FXS
===============

/etc/init.d/voip starts both lines automatically. Audio defaults match the
tested RAM setup: echo cancellation, mild noise reduction and 50% playback.
Use /etc/config/xr500v-voip for enable/disable and audio options, then restart
voip with handsets on-hook. Do not run the old laboratory voip-dual alongside.

Phone1: /dev/xr500v-voice, SIP LAN:5060, ctrl_tcp loopback:4444.
Phone2: /dev/xr500v-voice1, SIP LAN:5062, ctrl_tcp loopback:4445.
Each manager owns its ctrl_tcp connection. Diagnostics go through the root-only
FIFO under /var/run/xr500v-voip/phoneN/control; never displace it with nc.

Persistent configuration:
  /etc/xr500v-voip/config                 common baresip settings
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/accounts.template  @LANIP@ substitution
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/accounts        optional exact account-file override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/config          optional per-line settings override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/contacts        optional contacts override
  /etc/xr500v-voip/phoneN/speeddial       optional URI to dial on lifting

Accounts/config are rendered privately into /var/run/xr500v-voip/phoneN;
stored files are not overwritten. No test speed dial or ISP credentials are
shipped. Defaults are local SIP accounts phone1/phone2, regint=0 (no registrar).
ISP voice provisioning and a general outgoing digit dialplan are not provided
by this package. A successful local two-phone call does not validate ISP voice.

UCI options (/etc/config/xr500v-voip)
-------------------------------------
config voip 'globals'
  ring_on_ms / ring_off_ms   ring cadence in ms (default 1000 / 4000, AR POTS)
  ring_prefeed_ms            active feed before the first burst (default 1500, 0-5000;
                             0 rings straight out of low-power standby)
  emergency_numbers          dialed immediately, without the 4 s inter-digit wait;
                             empty by default because it is per country and a
                             longer number starting with one of them can no longer
                             be dialed (Argentina: '911 100 101 103 105 106 107 135
                             142 144')
  caller_id                  off (default) | v23 | bell202: FSK caller ID in the
                             pause after the first burst (date and time, the
                             number or "private"/"unavailable", the name if the
                             network sends one). The phone's clock is set from
                             it, so set the router's time zone. EXPERIMENTAL:
                             the phone it was tested with did not decode it yet

  call_waiting               1 (default) | 0: a call ringing in during a
                             conversation gets the call waiting tone (425 Hz,
                             0.2/0.2/0.2 s every 5 s) instead of busy
  flash_min_ms/flash_max_ms  hook flash window, the R key (80/750; the Movistar
                             AR HGU uses 120/750). A longer on-hook hangs up,
                             so with call waiting on, hanging up takes
                             flash_max_ms instead of 450 ms

config line 'phoneN'
  enabled        0/1
  account_mode   local | provider | custom        (phone1 and phone2)
                 shared                            (phone2 ONLY)
  idle_feed      standby (default) | active
  dial_mode      none | keypad | hotline
  network, username, domain, auth_user, proxy, transport, register,
  reg_interval, dtmf, hotline, password            (see LuCI -> Telephony)

Call waiting (globals.call_waiting)
-----------------------------------
  * R during the tone answers the waiting call; the first one goes on hold
    (SIP re-INVITE, the far end sees "on hold").
  * R with one call on hold swaps the two.
  * Hanging up with a call waiting rings the phone for it; with a call on hold
    it rings back and lifting resumes that call.
  * If the far end of the call being talked on hangs up, a held call is
    resumed at once; a waiting one stays waiting (R answers it).
  * A waiting call not answered within 30 s is rejected.
The network must deliver the second INVITE: an operator whose call waiting
service is off for the line answers the second caller busy itself (Movistar AR
did on 2026-09-26). scripts/voip-cw-robot.sh places a local second call.

Keys pressed during a call (IVR menus) are detected in the handset audio and
sent the way the account's dtmf option says (rtpevent = RFC 4733, the
default; info = SIP INFO; auto = RFC 4733 if the far end offered it). The
tone itself is muted in the voice sent meanwhile. XR500V_INCALL_DTMF=0 in the
baresip environment turns this off (the tones then go in-band, as before).

account_mode=shared on phone2: the shared ring group
----------------------------------------------------
Both jacks become ONE line, served by phone1's SIP account:
  * an incoming call rings BOTH bells and blinks BOTH PHONE LEDs;
  * the first handset lifted takes the call, the other bell stops and its LED
    goes off; lifting the other handset during the call is ignored (logged
    once), it does not join, take over or end the call;
  * lifting either handset while idle gives dial tone and dials on the phone1
    account (dial_mode/dialing settings of phone1);
  * phone2 gets NO SIP account, NO baresip and NO call manager: only
    phone1-sip and phone1-manager run. Applying shared while phone1 has no
    account_mode is refused and logged; phone2 is then not started at all.
  * the 1# / 2# local intercom DOES NOT EXIST in this mode -- there is no
    second account to call. dial_mode must be "none" on the shared port.
The phone1 manager publishes the answering jack in
/var/run/xr500v-voip/phone1/audio_device (0600, replaced atomically); the
baresip "xr500v" module reads it through XR500V_DEVICE_FILE when a call starts
and opens /dev/xr500v-voice (jack phone1) or /dev/xr500v-voice1 (jack phone2).

idle_feed: silence vs. instant dial tone
----------------------------------------
  standby (default)  On hook, the line is parked in SS_LOWPOWER (SLIC STATE
                     0x0c) with the LPM hook comparator armed. The router's
                     DC-DC buck-boost runs at light load and is SILENT. Cost:
                     the line has to be raised to the active feed when the
                     handset is lifted, so dial tone is a few tens of ms late
                     and the handset can make a faint "clack" on the edge.
  active             The line stays at the active feed (STATE 0x23) while on
                     hook: dial tone is immediate and there is no transition
                     click. Cost: the buck-boost hisses ("tssss") into the
                     earpiece while idle, which is exactly the noise standby
                     was introduced to remove. Use it per line only if the
                     delay bothers you more than the hiss.
Set per line: xr500v-voip.phone1.idle_feed / xr500v-voip.phone2.idle_feed, or
LuCI -> Telephony -> Configuration -> Line feed.
Related driver experiment: insmod econet-slic dual_soft_offhook=1 makes the
standby->active edge two-step (switcher LP, active, 20 ms, switcher HP) to
soften that click. Default 0 = current behaviour. It is skipped automatically
while the other jack is powered, because the switcher rail is shared.

Legacy single-line accounts.template and contacts are retained for backup;
custom legacy account settings must be explicitly mapped to phoneN before
using the new dual service. Check real NAND configuration before sysupgrade.
The package's keep.d entry preserves the whole configuration directory.

The audio module statically links SpeexDSP; see speexdsp.COPYING for its notice.
RAM-only checkpoints 0c4839c and 75b4cd8 are retained in the project for rollback.
